<h2>Does exercise raise or lower cortisol?</h2>
<p><strong>Exercise can change cortisol, but "lower" is not always the goal.</strong></p>
<p>A short rise during a hard workout can be part of your body's normal response to effort. It doesn't mean exercise is harming you, or that your cortisol stays high all day.</p>
<p><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18787373/">Hill and colleagues studied exercise intensity and cortisol in the blood</a>. Their study shows why how hard you work, and when samples are taken, matter. It doesn't give one cortisol-lowering workout for everyone.</p>

<h2>Walking, cardio, yoga and strength training</h2>
<p><strong>Choose movement you can repeat and recover from.</strong></p>
<p>Walking, cycling, swimming, strength work and gentler movement can suit different abilities and tastes. You don't need to judge every workout by a hormone score.</p>
<p>For adults, the <a href="https://odphp.health.gov/our-work/nutrition-physical-activity/physical-activity-guidelines/current-guidelines">US Physical Activity Guidelines</a> recommend:</p>
<ul>
  <li>150–300 minutes of moderate aerobic activity a week, or the same amount of vigorous activity, <strong>plus</strong></li>
  <li>muscle-strengthening activity on at least two days.</li>
</ul>
<p>Start with less if you need to. These guidelines are about overall health, not a guaranteed cortisol drop.</p>

<h3>Walking and easy cardio</h3>
<p>A short walk is a practical start when you're busy or getting back into activity. Pick a pace and length that fit you right now.</p>
<p>Track how you feel and whether you can keep it up. Don't promise yourself a certain percentage drop in cortisol.</p>

<ZenTip image="/app-ui/zen/zen-walk.webp">
  Busy day? Put a <strong>short walk</strong> in your calendar like any other plan, so it actually happens.
</ZenTip>

<h3>Yoga and gentler movement</h3>
<p>If you enjoy yoga, stretching or a gentle class, build it around comfort and doing it often.</p>
<p>"Restorative", "power" and "hot" classes ask very different things of your body. The name alone doesn't tell you what will happen to your hormones.</p>

<h3>Resistance (strength) training</h3>
<p>Plan sessions you can manage and leave time to recover.</p>
<p>If a program keeps leaving you unable to get through a normal day, rethink the load with a qualified trainer or clinician. More exercise isn't automatically a better answer to feeling stressed.</p>

<h2>Is HIIT bad for cortisol?</h2>
<p><strong>High-intensity interval training (HIIT) is not automatically bad.</strong></p>
<p>The useful question is whether it fits your training history, your other activity and your recovery. A wearable score can't set the right number of hard sessions for everyone.</p>

<h2>When should I rethink my training load?</h2>
<p><strong>Ongoing tiredness, worse performance, poor sleep or mood changes deserve attention.</strong> But none of them points to a hormone problem on its own.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23247672/">ECSS/ACSM overtraining consensus</a> describes overtraining as a complex diagnosis. Other causes have to be ruled out. There is no single generally accepted biomarker (lab marker) test that makes this diagnosis.</p>
<p>Don't treat a fixed HRV percentage, a few days of resting heart rate change, or an app score as proof of overtraining. Get professional help if symptoms keep going and you don't know why.</p>

